        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: जल्दी",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "जल्दी (jaldī) — \"quickly, soon\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "From Persian jald, 'brisk, quick', itself an Arabic word, with the ending Persian hangs on such words to make a quality-word. So जल्दी (jaldī) began as 'quickness' and ended up doing the work of 'quickly' and 'soon' at once."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Hindi still keeps both jobs alive. जल्दी (jaldī) on its own tells someone to hurry; जल्दी (jaldī) beside a plan means it will happen before long. The word does not distinguish speed from nearness in time, and speakers hear which one from the sentence around it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two."
            }
          ]
        },
